Using the command cat - does not return anything because - is a special character in Linux. Most commands interpret - as "read from standard input (Keyboard)" rather than a file name, so if you type cat -, it won't look for a file — it'll just sit and wait for you to type something. By adding ./; you're explicitly telling the shell:

"This is a file path, not a flag or special input — look for a file literally named - right here."


